# Licensing

This repository holds two kinds of material under two licences, plus one
directory that belongs to somebody else.

| What | Licence | Full text |
|---|---|---|
| `spec/`, `README.md`, `PLAN.md`, `CONTRIBUTING.md`, `AGENTS.md`, `CHANGELOG.md` — the prose | **CC-BY-4.0** | [LICENSES/CC-BY-4.0.txt](LICENSES/CC-BY-4.0.txt) |
| `tools/`, `gen/`, `data/`, `.github/`, `zensical.toml` — the code, the generators and the reference files they produce | **BSD-3-Clause** | [LICENSES/BSD-3-Clause.txt](LICENSES/BSD-3-Clause.txt) |
| `root/` — a submodule, not content of this repository | ROOT's own **LGPL-2.1-or-later** | `root/LICENSE` |
| `spec/05-rntuple/BinaryFormatSpecification.md` — see below | not ours to licence | — |

Copyright (c) 2026, Andres Rios Tascon.

## Why the split

The specification is a document meant to let other projects describe the format
the same way. CC-BY-4.0 lets them quote and adapt it with attribution.

The reference files are meant to be vendored as test vectors by readers in any
language, together with their `case.toml` assertions and the generators that made
them. BSD-3-Clause makes that unambiguous, including for a closed-source
implementation. If you copy a fixture, copy its `case.toml` with it, since the
assertions are what make the bytes useful; [CONTRIBUTING.md](CONTRIBUTING.md)
explains the format.

## The one exception

`spec/05-rntuple/BinaryFormatSpecification.md` is a **byte-for-byte copy of
ROOT's own RNTuple specification**. It is tracked so that this project's RNTuple
errata refer to a fixed text, and `tools/sync_rntuple.py --check` fails in CI if
it drifts. It is the ROOT project's document under ROOT's licence, not ours, and
no correction is ever made inside it. Corrections go in
`spec/05-rntuple/ERRATA.md`, which is ours and is CC-BY-4.0 like the rest of
`spec/`.

Every other file in `spec/` cites ROOT's source by `path:line` rather than
copying it. Where a short excerpt appears, it is quoted as evidence for a claim
about the format, is attributed to the file and line it came from, and remains
under ROOT's licence.

## Third-party corpora are never committed

The specification is measured against files other people wrote: ROOT's own
regression suite `root/roottest/`, uproot's regression corpus
(scikit-hep-testdata), go-hep's `groot/testdata`, files published at
root.cern and CERN Open Data, and, in a survey, rntuple-validation, KM3NeT's
test data and UnROOT's samples. **None of their files is committed here, and
none ever will be.** `root/roottest/` is read in place, inside the submodule; the
others are fetched into the untracked `build/` on demand. What is committed is a
manifest of their digests (`gen/foreign/MANIFEST.sha256`, `gen/cern/`) and the
reasons each file is listed.

Two of those sources, roottest and rntuple-validation, are under **LGPL-2.1**,
which the BSD-3-Clause licence of `data/` could not carry. The rule also keeps
every reference file's provenance simple: each one is the output of a generator
in `gen/`, run against the pinned ROOT or written by `tools/rootwrite.py`.
`tools/test_provenance.py` enforces both on every push. It fails on a tracked
ROOT file that no case generates, and on any tracked file byte-identical to a
file in `root/roottest/`.

When a third-party file teaches this project a fact, the fact is recorded by a
new fixture that reproduces it: `data/rntuple/map.root` does this for the
`std::map` finding, and `data/serialization/unframed-records.root` for the
unframed `TDatime` record first seen in go-hep's corpus.

## Citing this

[CITATION.cff](CITATION.cff) has the machine-readable form. Attribution under
CC-BY-4.0 is satisfied by naming the project and linking to it:

> ROOT I/O Specification, <https://ariostas.github.io/root-io-spec/>

## Not affiliated with ROOT

This is an independent description of a format, written from ROOT's published
source. It is not endorsed by, released by, or a product of the ROOT project or
CERN. Where this specification and ROOT disagree, ROOT is right. `spec/index.md`
states this, and it is a licensing statement as much as a technical one.
